  },
  "reading": "<p>The swarm searched the goals faster and travelled between them more slowly, and for both pilots the first outweighed the second.</p><p>At the goals the swarm took between a third and a half of the single drone's time, which is what ten cameras looking round a ring of buildings at once should buy; between goals it took longer, the formation's price in narrow streets, as the simulation studies predicted. In total it came out ahead for both pilots, narrowly for ERKS and clearly for ALEX. With two pilots this is a direction, not a result: one long leg (ERKS's third swarm run) carries much of the swarm's travel time, and ALEX, the pilot with the larger lead, flew the swarm second, so practice and condition are confounded.</p>",
  "data_notes": [
